  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In North Macedonia, the Organisation of Employers of Macedonia (OEM) and the Business Confederation of Macedonia (BCM) conducted advocacy activities based on assessments of the business environment.

Between Oct-Nov 2022, the OEM performed a study that gathered the views of 125 companies on the business impacts of the energy crisis. Drawing on the survey findings, the OEM proposed two packages of support measures. The first focused on subsidies for companies buying electricity on the liberalized market, and the second on support measures for companies transitioning to renewable energy production. These proposals were discussed with high level officials, including the Deputy Prime Minister, during a round table held on 15 Jan 2022, with further regular discussions taking place within the Economic and Social Council (ESC). As a result, in Oct 2022 the government temporarily capped the electricity price at &#x20ac;80/MWh for companies producing basic food items. The OEM continued to engage with the policy makers by developing and advocating for additional recommendations on energy subsidy mechanisms discussed at an event on 9 June 2023.

The BCM, in partnership with a research company, conducted an assessment of how the business environment impacts the digitalisation of business, and analyzed the internal capabilities of companies in terms of leveraging digital technology. The assessment involved a survey conducted between April-May 2023 that collected the views of 130 companies. This evidence was used to formulate recommendations aimed at creating a business environment that enables MSEs to embrace digital opportunities. These proposals were discussed with high-level government officials, including the Minister of Information Society and Administration, during an event held in Dec 2023. Subsequent meetings were held with relevant authorities and a visibility campaign disseminated the research findings and promoted the recommendations. The ILO assisted the OEM in designing a tailored methodological approach for the assessment of the business impacts of the energy crisis and provided further external expertise to conduct a survey among companies, analyze the data and develop recommendations. The ILO contributed to the research, as well as to the recommendations that stemmed from both the report and the follow up policy position paper. The ILO also supported a visibility campaign which produced tangible results, with 57 articles published by various media outlets on the topic of the research.

To support the BCM, the ILO developed a comprehensive methodology for assessing the impacts of business environment on digital transformation of companies. A particular focus was placed on the regulatory and policy framework, the availability and affordability of digital infrastructure, technologies and e-commerce platforms, access to finance, and the supply of digital labour. In addition, the methodology was designed to make an in-depth analysis of the internal company capabilities to adopt digital technology. The ILO provided external expertise to the BCM to conduct the research and contributed with substantial inputs to the final report. Additionally, the ILO supported a visibility campaign with research findings largely discussed in the public media. 

Both EBMOs were closely involved in each stage of the research process, thereby enhancing their own analytical capacity for conducting future research. In particular, the ILO acquainted the EBMOs with various research methodologies, organised knowledge sharing sessions between external experts and the EBMO staff, engaged them in the design of questionnaires, survey data collection and review of the reports. Both EBMOs co-facilitated the focus group discussions with their members. Furthermore, the EBMOs enhanced their advocacy capacity by being actively engaged in the promotion of research recommendations based on clear guidance provided by the ILO.</narrative>
   </description>
